reference": -9, "id": "28"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/p-oZ-SPL794/0.webp", "preference": -8, "id": "29"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEbCKgBEF5IVfKriqkDDggBFQAAiEIYAXABwAEG&rs=AOn4CLBghPw0GFgfPM8_-EjPOI3qGn5V2Q", "height": 94, "width": 168, "preference": -7, "id": "30", "resolution": "168x94"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEbCMQBEG5IVfKriqkDDggBFQAAiEIYAXABwAEG&rs=AOn4CLDAF2eTHGO5FQEFxoQjJMpvJt35kg", "height": 110, "width": 196, "preference": -7, "id": "31", "resolution": "196x110"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEcCPYBEIoBSFXyq4qpAw4IARUAAIhCGAFwAcABBg==&rs=AOn4CLD4ZjzAnyHd90t964Dw1im6y3zuyg", "height": 138, "width": 246, "preference": -7, "id": "32", "resolution": "246x138"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEcCNACELwBSFXyq4qpAw4IARUAAIhCGAFwAcABBg==&rs=AOn4CLAjIafITxAl78a1VlzjfB7NY8flmQ", "height": 188, "width": 336, "preference": -7, "id": "33", "resolution": "336x188"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.jpg", "height": 360, "width": 480, "preference": -7, "id": "34", "resolution": "480x360"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.webp", "preference": -6, "id": "35"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/sddefault.jpg", "preference": -5, "id": "36"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/p-oZ-SPL794/sddefault.webp", "preference": -4, "id": "37"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hq720.jpg", "preference": -3, "id": "38"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/p-oZ-SPL794/hq720.webp", "preference": -2, "id": "39"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/maxresdefault.jpg", "preference": -1, "id": "40"}, {"url": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi_webp/p-oZ-SPL794/maxresdefault.webp", "preference": 0, "id": "41"}], "thumbnail": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/p-oZ-SPL794/hqdefault.jpg", "description": "(13 Sep 2007) \r\n1. Wide exterior of International Automobile Federation (FIA)\r\n2. Mid-shot of sign reading: Federation Internationale de L'Automobile\r\n3. Close-up of FIA sign\r\n4. FIA President, Max Mosley arrives at hearing surrounded by cameras\r\n5. President of Formula One Management (FOM), Bernie Ecclestone arrives and enters FIA building\r\n6. Vice President of Mercedes Benz Motor Sport, Norbut Haug arrives surrounded by cameras\r\n7. Ferrari Team Principal, Jean Todt arriving \r\n8. Journalists waiting outside FIA\r\n9. McLaren Team Principal and Chairman and CEO of Mclaren Group, Ron Dennis arrives surrounded by cameras\r\n10. McLaren test driver, Lewis Hamilton arriving\r\n11. McLaren test driver, Pedro Martinez De La Rosa arriving\r\n12. Men pulling boxes of documents out of a trunk of car\r\n13. Various of boxes of documents in front of FIA entrance\r\n14. Pan from IFA building to press waiting outside\r\nSTORYLINE:\r\nThe World Motor Sports Council was expected to decide on Thursday whether to sanction the McLaren team in the spy scandal that has rocked Formula One. \r\nIn a hearing on Thursday before the Paris-based organisation, McLaren is to face new evidence in the investigation into the team's alleged use of secret technical documents belonging to F1 rival Ferrari. \r\nIf found guilty, McLaren could face stiff punishment, ranging from expulsion from the championship this season and next to deduction of points from the team or drivers Lewis Hamilton and Fernando Alonso.\r\nThe case broke open in July when a 780-page technical dossier on Ferrari cars was found at the home of McLaren's chief designer, Mike Coughlan, who was later suspended. \r\nFerrari mechanic Nigel Stepney, who allegedly supplied the documents, was fired. \r\nThe World Motor Sport Council ruled in July that McLaren was guilty of fraudulent conduct for possessing the Ferrari documents but did not punish the team because there was insufficient evidence the material was misused. \r\nHowever, the council warned that McLaren could be kicked out of the 2007 and 2008 series if it is found in the future that the information has been used \"to the detriment of the championship.\"\r\nWorld governing body FIA (International Automobile Federation) announced last week it was calling a new hearing of the council after \"new evidence\" had emerged.\r\nMcLaren said it would make \"a strong set of submissions\" in its defence, but declined to comment further until the hearing.\r\nFIA president Max Mosley sent letters to Alonso, Hamilton and test driver Pedro De La Rosa on 31 August, saying the sport's regulator had been told that \"one or more McLaren drivers may be in possession ... of written evidence relevant to this investigation.\"\r\nMosley asked the three drivers to cooperate \"in the interests of the sport and the championship\" and offered them amnesty in return. \r\nMosley also wrote that \"serious consequences would follow\" if they were later found to \"have withheld any potentially relevant information.\"\r\nAlonso has reportedly submitted evidence to FIA.\r\nThe Times of London newspaper reported on Wednesday that the case against McLaren consists of a 166-page dossier that includes e-mail exchanges between De la Rosa and Alonso as well as details of phone and text message traffic between Coughlan and Stepney supplied to FIA by authorities in Italy.\r\n\r\nFind out more about AP Archive: http://www.aparchive.com/HowWeWork \r\nTwitter: https://twitter.com/AP_Archive \r\nFacebook: https://www.facebook.com/APArchives ​​\r\nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/APNews/\r\n\r\n\r\nYou can license this story through AP Archive: http://www.aparchive.com/metadata/youtube/121b5cc20447ecfb1dfc0917be1ab27f", "channel_id": "UCHTK-2W11Vh1V4uwofOfR4w", "channel_url": "https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCHTK-2W11Vh1V4uwofOfR4w", "duration": 145, "view_count": 435, "age_limit": 0, "webpage_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p-oZ-SPL794", "categories": ["News & Politics"], "tags": ["AP Archive", "536411", "121b5cc20